---
const hisFields = ["Photo", "Title", "Category", "Notes for me"];
const myStudio = [
  "Orderable documents",
  "Conditional fields",
  "Image hotspots",
  "Full schema control",
];
---

<!-- Designing for the Owner -->
<section class="mb-24">
  <div class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-2">
      Designing for the Owner
    </h2>
  </div>

  <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4 max-w-3xl">
    Picking the CMS: I shipped the wrong one first.
  </h3>
  <div class="space-y-5 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed text-lg max-w-3xl">
    <p>
      I started on Decap CMS (formerly Netlify CMS). It hit two walls. It's
      git-based, so his content lived in the repo and every upload was a commit
      that triggered a full Netlify rebuild: slow for him, and it burned build
      minutes just to post a photo. And the studio, the part he'd actually
      touch, was clunky on a phone, his only way to post. By the time I admitted
      it, I'd already moved the project from GitHub Pages to Netlify and
      migrated the DNS. I scrapped it and moved to Sanity anyway. Sunk cost is
      still a cost, but shipping the wrong tool is worse.
    </p>
    <p>
      I picked Sanity partly because its studio is built to work on a phone,
      which Decap's wasn't. In practice it was okay on mobile, not good enough
      for him, and that gap is what told me no off-the-shelf studio was ever
      going to be his tool. What Sanity gave me that Decap couldn't: content
      lives in a hosted dataset instead of my repo, so a new photo shows up with
      no rebuild; built-in image hotspots and cropping; drag-to-reorder
      galleries; and real conditional fields.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4 max-w-3xl">
    I modeled the content, then built him a door into it.
  </h3>
  <div class="space-y-5 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed text-lg max-w-3xl">
    <p>
      On my side, I modeled the content properly: orderable gallery documents so
      I can drag his work into the order he wants, and conditional fields so the
      form stays clean. The subcategory dropdown only appears for the categories
      that actually have them (concrete and landscaping) and shows the right
      sub-options for each. That part proves I architect content models, not
      just call an API.
    </p>
    <p>
      Even a clean studio is a developer's mental model, not a contractor's, so
      I didn't hand it to him. I built a separate page at <code
        class="font-mono text-[0.92em] text-violet-700 bg-violet-50 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded"
        >/upload</code
      >, in Spanish, that does one job: it shows him what he needs and nothing
      else.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- His page vs my studio -->
  <div class="my-6 grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-5 max-w-3xl">
    <div
      class="rounded-2xl border border-violet-200 bg-gradient-to-br from-violet-50 to-white p-5 shadow-sm"
    >
      <p
        class="text-[10px] font-bold text-violet-700 uppercase tracking-widest mb-3"
      >
        What he sees · /upload
      </p>
      <ul class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
        {
          hisFields.map((f) => (
            <li class="text-sm font-medium text-violet-800 bg-white border border-violet-200 rounded-full px-3 py-1">
              {f}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-2xl border border-gray-200 bg-white p-5 shadow-sm">
      <p
        class="text-[10px] font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest mb-3"
      >
        What I keep · Sanity Studio
      </p>
      <ul class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
        {
          myStudio.map((f) => (
            <li class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-700 bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-full px-3 py-1">
              {f}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 italic max-w-3xl mb-6">
    Same data, two interfaces. The form he'll actually use, the studio I need.
  </p>

  <div class="space-y-5 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed text-lg max-w-3xl">
    <p>
      He fills it out like an Instagram post, the muscle memory he already had,
      and submits. Behind it, <code
        class="font-mono text-[0.92em] text-violet-700 bg-violet-50 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded"
        >/api/upload</code
      > pushes the image to Sanity's CDN, creates the <code
        class="font-mono text-[0.92em] text-violet-700 bg-violet-50 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded"
        >galleryItem</code
      > document, and copies his title into the alt text so the site stays accessible
      without him ever thinking about it. The full studio is still there for me.
      He never has to see it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4 max-w-3xl">
    The details that made it his.
  </h3>
  <div class="space-y-5 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed text-lg max-w-3xl">
    <p>
      That notes field is doing more than it looks. It's an async channel
      between us. He leaves a note ("put this one first," or "this is the
      Hendersons' backyard"), I see it in Sanity, I handle it. No phone call, no
      scheduling, no "are you free." The tool absorbed the back-and-forth that
      used to be a conversation.
    </p>
    <p>
      One real-world snag: he shoots on an iPhone, so every photo lands as HEIC,
      which browsers won't render. The upload page converts it to JPEG right on
      his phone, before it ever uploads, so he sends a smaller file and my
      server never has to touch it. He just sees his work show up on the site.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p
    class="mt-8 max-w-3xl text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900 leading-snug border-l-4 border-amber-400 pl-5"
  >
    The result is the thing I set out to build: he posts his own work, from his
    phone, in his language, and the site updates itself. He doesn't need me to
    keep his business online.
  </p>
</section>
